WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:12.747 --> 00:00:14.957
All right, I'm going to go out
on a limb here.

00:00:14.999 --> 00:00:20.421
I'm going to say that every single
one of us in this room

00:00:20.463 --> 00:00:22.465
made drawings when we were little.

00:00:22.465 --> 00:00:23.632
Yes?

00:00:23.632 --> 00:00:25.134
Yes? OK.

00:00:25.134 --> 00:00:30.014
And maybe around the age of like,
four or five or something like that,

00:00:30.056 --> 00:00:31.474
you might have been drawing,

00:00:31.515 --> 00:00:34.643
and a grown-up came over
and looked over your shoulder and said,

00:00:34.685 --> 00:00:35.853
"What's that?"

00:00:35.895 --> 00:00:38.189
And you said, "It's a face."

00:00:38.189 --> 00:00:39.815
And they said,

00:00:39.815 --> 00:00:42.610
"That's not really what a face looks like.

00:00:42.651 --> 00:00:44.195
This is what a face looks like."

00:00:44.195 --> 00:00:46.155
And they proceeded to draw this.

00:00:46.197 --> 00:00:49.658
Circle, two almonds for some eyes,

00:00:49.658 --> 00:00:53.162
this upside-down seven
situation we have here,

00:00:53.162 --> 00:00:55.081
and then a curved line.

00:00:55.581 --> 00:00:56.957
But guess what?

00:00:56.999 --> 00:01:00.377
This doesn't really look
that much like a face, OK?

00:01:00.419 --> 00:01:01.587
It's an icon.

00:01:01.587 --> 00:01:03.089
It's visual shorthand,

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:06.842
and it's how we look
at so much of our world today.

00:01:06.842 --> 00:01:10.137
See, we have so much information
coming at us all the time,

00:01:10.137 --> 00:01:13.015
that our brains literally
can't process it,

00:01:13.057 --> 00:01:15.684
and we fill in the world with patterns.

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:21.023
Much of what we see
is our own expectations.

00:01:21.899 --> 00:01:23.192
All right.

00:01:23.192 --> 00:01:24.944
I'm going to show you a little trick

00:01:24.985 --> 00:01:29.031
to rewire your brain into looking again.

00:01:29.406 --> 00:01:32.660
Did you all get an envelope
that says "do not open" on it?

00:01:32.701 --> 00:01:35.162
Grab that envelope, it's time to open it.

00:01:35.871 --> 00:01:40.334
Inside should be
a piece of paper and a pencil.

00:01:41.585 --> 00:01:43.087
Once you have that all prepped,

00:01:43.087 --> 00:01:45.047
please turn to somebody next to you.

00:01:45.089 --> 00:01:47.967
Ideally, somebody you don't know.

00:01:49.510 --> 00:01:51.637
Yeah, we're doing this, people,

00:01:51.679 --> 00:01:52.930
we're doing this.

00:01:52.972 --> 00:01:54.306
(Laughs)

00:01:54.974 --> 00:01:56.100
Great.

00:01:56.517 --> 00:01:58.018
Everybody find a partner?

00:01:58.060 --> 00:01:59.478
OK, now look back at me.

00:01:59.520 --> 00:02:01.397
OK, now look back at me.

00:02:01.856 --> 00:02:06.110
You are going to draw each other, OK?

00:02:06.152 --> 00:02:08.070
No, no, no, no, wait, wait, wait, wait.

00:02:08.112 --> 00:02:10.823
I promise this is not
about doing a good drawing, OK?

00:02:10.865 --> 00:02:12.449
That's not what we're doing here,

00:02:12.491 --> 00:02:14.285
we're looking, this is about looking.

00:02:14.285 --> 00:02:16.996
Everybody's going to be terrible,
I promise, don't worry.

00:02:17.037 --> 00:02:19.832
You're going to draw each other
with two very simple rules.

00:02:19.874 --> 00:02:22.835
One, you are never going to lift
your pencil up off the paper.

00:02:22.835 --> 00:02:24.044
One continuous line.

00:02:24.086 --> 00:02:25.462
No, no, trust me here.

00:02:25.504 --> 00:02:27.965
This is about looking, OK?

00:02:28.007 --> 00:02:30.176
So one continuous line
never lift the pencil.

00:02:30.217 --> 00:02:31.385
Number two,

00:02:31.427 --> 00:02:36.932
never, ever, ever look down
at the paper you're drawing on, OK?

00:02:36.974 --> 00:02:38.350
Yes, it's about looking.

00:02:38.392 --> 00:02:41.312
So keep looking at the person
you're drawing.

00:02:41.729 --> 00:02:45.858
Now put your pencil down in
the middle of the paper, OK?

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.067
Look up at your partner.

00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:49.445
Look at the inside of one of their eyes.

00:02:49.445 --> 00:02:50.654
Doesn't matter which one.

00:02:50.696 --> 00:02:52.406
That's where you're going to start.

00:02:52.406 --> 00:02:53.574
Ready?

00:02:53.574 --> 00:02:54.742
Deep breath.

00:02:54.783 --> 00:02:55.951
(Inhales)

00:02:55.951 --> 00:02:57.578
And begin.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:00.831
Now, just draw but notice where you are,

00:03:00.831 --> 00:03:03.292
you're starting there
and you see there is a corner,

00:03:03.334 --> 00:03:04.710
maybe there's a curve there.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.129
Notice those little lines, the eyelashes.

00:03:07.171 --> 00:03:09.965
People are wearing masks,
some aren't, just work with that.

00:03:09.965 --> 00:03:11.550
Now just go slow.

00:03:11.592 --> 00:03:14.553
Pay attention and draw what you see.

00:03:14.595 --> 00:03:16.805
And don't look down.

00:03:16.805 --> 00:03:18.474
Just keep going.

00:03:18.474 --> 00:03:19.475
(Murmuring)

00:03:19.516 --> 00:03:22.478
And just five more seconds.

00:03:22.478 --> 00:03:24.855
And stop.

00:03:24.897 --> 00:03:26.857
Look down at your beautiful drawings.

00:03:26.899 --> 00:03:28.484
(Laughter)

00:03:28.484 --> 00:03:29.693
Right?

00:03:29.693 --> 00:03:33.280
Show your partner
their incredible portrait.

00:03:33.614 --> 00:03:35.574
It's so good, right?

00:03:35.574 --> 00:03:36.951
I want to see them.

00:03:36.951 --> 00:03:38.118
Hold them up.

00:03:38.160 --> 00:03:39.453
Can you guys hold them up?

00:03:39.495 --> 00:03:41.705
Hold up, everybody.

00:03:41.997 --> 00:03:43.374
Oh my gosh.

00:03:43.415 --> 00:03:44.750
Are you kidding me?

00:03:44.750 --> 00:03:46.418
You all are amazing.

00:03:46.418 --> 00:03:48.379
OK, you can put your drawings back down,

00:03:48.379 --> 00:03:49.672
tuck them under,

00:03:49.713 --> 00:03:51.048
put them on the paper.

00:03:52.049 --> 00:03:53.217
That was wonderful.

00:03:53.259 --> 00:03:55.719
I mean, they're all terrible,
but they're wonderful.

00:03:55.719 --> 00:03:56.887
Why are they wonderful?

00:03:56.929 --> 00:03:59.473
Because you all just drew a face.

00:03:59.473 --> 00:04:00.683
You drew what you saw.

00:04:00.724 --> 00:04:03.644
You didn't draw what you think
a face looks like, right?

00:04:03.644 --> 00:04:07.398
You also just did something
that people rarely do.

00:04:07.690 --> 00:04:11.068
You just made intimate eye-to-eye,

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:15.197
face-to-face contact with someone
without shying away

00:04:15.239 --> 00:04:16.532
for almost a minute.

00:04:17.074 --> 00:04:19.618
Through drawing, you slowed down,

00:04:19.660 --> 00:04:21.537
you paid attention,

00:04:21.537 --> 00:04:24.081
you looked closely at someone

00:04:24.081 --> 00:04:26.917
and you let them look closely at you.

00:04:27.584 --> 00:04:28.794
Good job.

00:04:29.461 --> 00:04:31.547
I have found that drawing like this

00:04:31.588 --> 00:04:35.301
creates an immediate connection
like nothing else.

00:04:37.219 --> 00:04:38.387
Alright.

00:04:38.429 --> 00:04:43.809
So I call myself an illustrator
and a graphic journalist.

00:04:44.268 --> 00:04:46.020
I draw, I tell stories.

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.647
I spend time with people
looking and listening.

00:04:48.689 --> 00:04:51.150
And I take the words
of the people that I speak with

00:04:51.150 --> 00:04:54.320
and I put it together with drawings
that I do, mostly from life,

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:55.904
just like you all just did.

00:04:57.406 --> 00:05:00.451
I found that drawing like this
does a lot of things

00:05:00.451 --> 00:05:03.120
that photography can't do.

00:05:04.413 --> 00:05:07.333
So when somebody points
a camera at you, how do you feel?

00:05:07.374 --> 00:05:09.418
A little objectified, right?

00:05:09.960 --> 00:05:12.338
When I'm drawing,
I hold my sketchbook low

00:05:12.338 --> 00:05:16.258
and it keeps an open channel
between me and the person I'm drawing.

00:05:16.258 --> 00:05:19.470
A lot of time somebody will see me drawing
and they'll get curious.

00:05:19.470 --> 00:05:20.721
They'll come over to me,

00:05:20.763 --> 00:05:23.766
and a real, authentic conversation begins.

00:05:24.099 --> 00:05:25.601
Let me give you an example.

00:05:26.101 --> 00:05:27.895
So a while back,

00:05:27.895 --> 00:05:30.689
I wanted to do a drawn story

00:05:30.689 --> 00:05:35.152
about how the public library
serves our elders.

00:05:36.320 --> 00:05:40.657
But after spending a few days
kind of lurking around with a sketch pad,

00:05:40.657 --> 00:05:44.203
looking over older folks' shoulders
and asking them what they were reading,

00:05:44.203 --> 00:05:46.330
I wasn't really getting the story.

00:05:46.705 --> 00:05:48.916
Until I stumbled upon Leah.

00:05:49.583 --> 00:05:56.382
Leah is the first, and at the time
was the only, full-time social worker

00:05:56.423 --> 00:05:59.343
dedicated to a library in the nation.

00:05:59.968 --> 00:06:03.514
Turns out, public library
definitely serves our elders.

00:06:03.555 --> 00:06:07.518
It is also a social service
epicenter of a city.

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:09.269
This is Charles.

00:06:09.603 --> 00:06:10.854
Charles works with Leah.

00:06:10.896 --> 00:06:13.399
And he does outreach
within the library to folks

00:06:13.440 --> 00:06:15.317
who are experiencing homelessness.

00:06:15.359 --> 00:06:16.527
And he took me around,

00:06:16.568 --> 00:06:19.363
I carried my sketch pad
and I was drawing everything I saw,

00:06:19.363 --> 00:06:23.909
and he showed me a very different library
than I'd previously seen.

00:06:23.909 --> 00:06:28.789
So computers that I assumed
were for checking-out books,

00:06:28.789 --> 00:06:31.083
or, you know, looking at emails,

00:06:31.083 --> 00:06:35.796
were in fact a lifeline for folks
who are searching for jobs and housing.

00:06:36.630 --> 00:06:39.091
The sinks in the public restroom,

00:06:39.091 --> 00:06:44.096
they are a laundromat and showers
for folks who are sleeping on the street.

00:06:44.138 --> 00:06:48.058
A library is a safe, quiet place

00:06:48.100 --> 00:06:52.563
where anybody can go and find resources

00:06:52.604 --> 00:06:55.107
and rest for free.

00:06:56.024 --> 00:07:01.363
See, the moment I stopped looking
for the story that I expected to see,

00:07:01.405 --> 00:07:05.576
an entirely new
and richer truth was revealed.

00:07:05.617 --> 00:07:09.246
I found this to be true with everything
and everyone I've ever drawn.

00:07:10.747 --> 00:07:13.250
OK, so I draw from life,
right, like you guys did.

00:07:13.292 --> 00:07:16.211
And so I built myself a mobile studio

00:07:16.253 --> 00:07:18.714
in the back of a swanky Honda Element --

00:07:19.798 --> 00:07:21.758
So that I could go anywhere,

00:07:21.758 --> 00:07:25.220
talk to anyone at any time and then draw
and paint and sleep in the back.

00:07:25.220 --> 00:07:27.222
It is very cozy.

00:07:27.931 --> 00:07:29.516
I was on the road in Utah,

00:07:29.516 --> 00:07:32.060
drawing and talking to people,

00:07:32.102 --> 00:07:36.398
when I spotted on the side of the road
a hand-painted wooden sign.

00:07:36.398 --> 00:07:38.984
It said "Bootmaker."

00:07:39.026 --> 00:07:40.319
I stopped.

00:07:40.319 --> 00:07:45.365
A tall, white, handlebar mustached man
wearing a cowboy shirt,

00:07:45.407 --> 00:07:48.076
opened the door and found me,

00:07:48.076 --> 00:07:51.538
a sketchbook-carrying, jumpsuit-wearing,
urban, lefty lesbian,

00:07:51.538 --> 00:07:53.499
smiling like, waving like a dork.

00:07:53.499 --> 00:07:56.126
(Laughter)

00:07:57.044 --> 00:08:00.214
When I spotted the stuffed cougar
on the wall behind him,

00:08:00.214 --> 00:08:04.051
this vegetarian thought she knew
all she needed to know

00:08:04.051 --> 00:08:05.719
about Don the bootmaker.

00:08:06.386 --> 00:08:08.055
But there we were.

00:08:08.096 --> 00:08:13.060
So I asked him if he'd just show me
quickly a little bit about his craft.

00:08:13.060 --> 00:08:14.394
He agreed.

00:08:14.811 --> 00:08:17.731
And we ended up spending
the whole day together,

00:08:17.731 --> 00:08:19.650
as I drew out Don in his workshop,

00:08:19.691 --> 00:08:24.363
and he told me about the sudden death
of his beloved wife,

00:08:24.404 --> 00:08:27.783
about his deep, deep grief,

00:08:27.824 --> 00:08:31.161
and about this hunting trip
that he was planning,

00:08:31.203 --> 00:08:34.081
and so looking forward
to taking with his son.

00:08:34.581 --> 00:08:38.085
Every tool in that shop held a story.

00:08:38.126 --> 00:08:40.963
And he was so, so happy

00:08:40.963 --> 00:08:45.425
to share it with somebody
who was genuinely curious and interested.

00:08:46.051 --> 00:08:47.261
By the end of the day,

00:08:47.261 --> 00:08:51.640
Don and I looked
very different to one another.

00:08:52.182 --> 00:08:53.559
And this drawing,

00:08:53.600 --> 00:08:56.228
which ended up in my visual column
in the New York Times

00:08:56.270 --> 00:08:58.647
or as Don likes to call it,
the fake-news media --

00:08:58.689 --> 00:09:01.775
(Laughter)

00:09:01.817 --> 00:09:06.530
now hangs framed on the wall
of his big game trophy room.

00:09:06.530 --> 00:09:08.615
(Laughter)

00:09:08.657 --> 00:09:12.452
(Applause)

00:09:12.452 --> 00:09:15.956
So I was getting ready to start
on a new drawn story

00:09:15.998 --> 00:09:17.249
when the pandemic hit.

00:09:17.249 --> 00:09:21.837
And overnight I was, like so many people,
just unable to do my job.

00:09:23.672 --> 00:09:29.094
It was my own mother who suggested
that I teach drawing to kids.

00:09:30.053 --> 00:09:32.055
Kids who were about to lose
their routines,

00:09:32.097 --> 00:09:33.557
be stuck at home,

00:09:33.599 --> 00:09:37.185
and to help give parents
a much needed short break.

00:09:38.770 --> 00:09:40.647
Now I'm trained as a social worker,

00:09:40.689 --> 00:09:42.899
but I'd never taught kids before.

00:09:43.400 --> 00:09:47.195
But the night before school
closures in San Francisco,

00:09:47.237 --> 00:09:48.488
I went on Instagram

00:09:48.530 --> 00:09:54.077
and announced that the next day
we'd try something called DrawTogether.

00:09:56.163 --> 00:09:57.539
10 am.

00:09:57.581 --> 00:10:02.169
I sat behind my drawing table
in my home studio

00:10:02.210 --> 00:10:04.880
and my wonderful wife
pointed an iPhone at me

00:10:04.921 --> 00:10:06.465
and pressed "Go live."

00:10:06.465 --> 00:10:09.343
And what I thought would be 100 kids,

00:10:09.343 --> 00:10:12.512
ended up being 12,000.

00:10:13.555 --> 00:10:16.350
All eager to draw a dog.

00:10:17.392 --> 00:10:18.727
The next day,

00:10:18.727 --> 00:10:21.396
14,000 kids came

00:10:21.438 --> 00:10:23.690
and we drew a tree,

00:10:23.690 --> 00:10:27.194
and that drawing exercise
that you all just did.

00:10:27.861 --> 00:10:32.157
What was supposed to be
five minutes for five days,

00:10:32.199 --> 00:10:34.743
ended up being 30 minutes a day,

00:10:34.743 --> 00:10:36.620
five days a week,

00:10:36.662 --> 00:10:38.330
for months.

00:10:38.955 --> 00:10:42.084
And yeah, we talked about line and shape

00:10:42.084 --> 00:10:43.752
and we learned about perspective

00:10:43.794 --> 00:10:45.212
and light and shadow.

00:10:45.545 --> 00:10:48.340
But what was really going on

00:10:48.340 --> 00:10:54.805
was we were actively looking our way
through a global catastrophe together.

00:10:56.682 --> 00:10:59.685
See, drawing slows us down.

00:11:00.769 --> 00:11:02.688
It keeps our hands moving

00:11:02.688 --> 00:11:04.856
so we can pay attention to things

00:11:04.898 --> 00:11:07.984
that we usually overlook
or that we ignore.

00:11:08.902 --> 00:11:13.407
Studies show that drawing
is one of the most effective ways

00:11:13.448 --> 00:11:16.201
for kids to process their emotions,

00:11:16.201 --> 00:11:18.120
and that includes trauma.

00:11:18.662 --> 00:11:22.124
It helps us talk about hard things.

00:11:23.041 --> 00:11:27.504
We say something in DrawTogether,
it sounds hokey, but it is true.

00:11:27.504 --> 00:11:29.798
Drawing is looking

00:11:29.840 --> 00:11:32.300
and looking is loving.

00:11:33.927 --> 00:11:37.597
If we can give kids the right
supportive environment,

00:11:37.597 --> 00:11:42.227
drawing helps them let go
of perfectionism and fear of failure

00:11:42.269 --> 00:11:44.479
so that they, unlike you and me,

00:11:44.479 --> 00:11:47.858
and especially those of us
who might have freaked out just a wee bit

00:11:47.899 --> 00:11:51.111
when I said earlier
we were going to draw, right?

00:11:51.153 --> 00:11:56.450
We can let go of these
harder self-judgments

00:11:56.450 --> 00:11:59.911
so we don't have to undo
them later in life.

00:12:02.873 --> 00:12:05.751
OK, I don't expect you all
to become drawers.

00:12:06.334 --> 00:12:11.339
But I do know that all of us,
kids, grownups, everyone in this room,

00:12:11.339 --> 00:12:14.551
we can all be better at looking.

00:12:15.177 --> 00:12:17.679
Because this is not a face.

00:12:17.971 --> 00:12:20.724
And when we live like this drawing,

00:12:20.724 --> 00:12:25.979
we miss out on all of the depth
and detail of the world

00:12:25.979 --> 00:12:27.856
and people around us.

00:12:28.398 --> 00:12:29.691
This is a face.

00:12:30.692 --> 00:12:32.194
And this is a face.

00:12:33.695 --> 00:12:36.114
And that is such a face.

00:12:36.156 --> 00:12:37.699
(Laughs)

00:12:38.658 --> 00:12:40.744
And these are faces.

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.248
And if you slow down, I promise,

00:12:45.290 --> 00:12:48.251
pay attention and really look.

00:12:49.336 --> 00:12:54.382
You will fall back in love
with the world and everyone in it.

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:57.469
And after the past few years we've had,

00:12:57.469 --> 00:13:03.683
I think we all desperately need a chance
to look closely at one another

00:13:03.725 --> 00:13:05.769
and at ourselves,

00:13:05.811 --> 00:13:09.189
and tell the real truth about what we see.

00:13:09.981 --> 00:13:11.149
Thank you.

00:13:11.149 --> 00:13:18.031
(Applause)

